          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old trauma patient has a heart rate of 120/min, blood pressure of 118/</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old trauma patient has a heart rate of 120/min, blood pressure of 118/80 mmHg, capillary refill of 3 seconds, and is anxious but alert. According to the ATLS haemorrhage classification, which class of blood loss does this most likely represent?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Class I — blood loss below 15%</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Class I typically shows minimal tachycardia and normal capillary refill. A heart rate of 120/min and a capillary refill of 3 seconds exceed what Class I produces.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Class II — blood loss of 15–30%</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Class II haemorrhage shows tachycardia, anxiety, and prolonged capillary refill, but blood pressure remains normal. Hypotension only appears at Class III when blood loss exceeds 30%.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Class III — blood loss of 30–40%</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Class III presents with frank hypotension. This patient's near-normal blood pressure of 118/80 mmHg rules out Class III, where compensatory mechanisms have begun to fail.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Class IV — blood loss exceeding 40%</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Class IV represents cardiovascular collapse with severe hypotension, marked tachycardia above 140/min, and obtundation. This patient's maintained blood pressure and alertness are incompatible with Class IV.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the ATLS classification. The key discriminator between Class II and Class III is the appearance of hypotension, which occurs only when blood loss exceeds 30%."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Class II is the hidden danger zone — significant blood loss with a deceptively normal blood pressure."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
