          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with warm, flushed skin, bounding pulse, and a blood pressure</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with warm, flushed skin, bounding pulse, and a blood pressure of 80/40 mmHg. Which type of shock does this haemodynamic pattern most likely represent?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypovolaemic shock</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypovolaemic shock presents with cold, pale, clammy peripheries because systemic vascular resistance rises as the body constricts vessels to compensate for lost volume.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cardiogenic shock</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cardiogenic shock presents with cold peripheries, raised jugular venous pressure, and signs of pulmonary congestion — not the warm, flushed pattern described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Distributive shock</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Warm, flushed peripheries indicate a dramatic fall in systemic vascular resistance — the hallmark of distributive shock. Septic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic shock all share this vasodilatory pattern.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Obstructive shock</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Obstructive shock, caused by tension pneumothorax or tamponade, mimics cardiogenic shock with cold peripheries and low cardiac output — not vasodilation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall the haemodynamic fingerprints: warm peripheries = low SVR = distributive. Cold peripheries = high SVR = hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, or obstructive."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The warm peripheries are the giveaway — only distributive shock drops systemic vascular resistance, producing vasodilation rather than vasoconstriction."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
                "type": "auto",
