          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with uncontrolled abdominal haemorrhage is being resuscitated. Accordi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with uncontrolled abdominal haemorrhage is being resuscitated. According to the principle of permissive hypotension, what systolic blood pressure target should you accept until haemostasis is achieved?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>120–130 mmHg to ensure adequate organ perfusion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Driving blood pressure this high before achieving haemostasis dislodges forming clot and worsens bleeding. The old approach of chasing a normal blood pressure with aggressive fluids has been superseded.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>100–110 mmHg as a safe intermediate target</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Even this target is too high in uncontrolled haemorrhage. Higher pressure forces more blood past the injury, dilutes clotting factors, and disrupts the fragile clots the body is trying to form.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>80–90 mmHg with a palpable radial pulse and alert mentation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Permissive hypotension accepts a systolic of 80–90 mmHg. A palpable radial pulse and an alert patient confirm adequate perfusion to vital organs while minimising clot disruption at the bleeding site.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>60–70 mmHg to minimise all bleeding pressure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This level risks critical organ hypoperfusion and irreversible ischaemic damage. Permissive hypotension balances reduced bleeding against maintaining enough perfusion to protect the brain and kidneys.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: in uncontrolled haemorrhage, target a systolic of 80–90 mmHg. The goal is a palpable radial pulse and an alert patient — not a normal blood pressure reading."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Permissive hypotension is a deliberate strategy — accept a lower pressure to preserve clot, and resuscitate to clinical signs rather than a textbook number."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
