          "label": "<p>A 70-year-old woman arrives warm, flushed, and confused with BP 85/50 mmHg, elev</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 70-year-old woman arrives warm, flushed, and confused with BP 85/50 mmHg, elevated lactate, and cloudy urine. Compared to managing a haemorrhagic shock patient, what is the most critical additional intervention for this patient?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Balanced blood-product transfusion in a 1:1:1 ratio</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The 1:1:1 transfusion protocol replaces lost blood components in major haemorrhage. This patient's warm, flushed peripheries and cloudy urine point to septic shock from a urinary source, not active blood loss.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Permissive hypotension with restricted fluid administration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Permissive hypotension is reserved for uncontrolled haemorrhage to prevent clot disruption. In septic shock, generous fluid resuscitation is needed, with vasopressors if hypotension persists.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Early broad-spectrum antibiotics with source control alongside fluid resuscitation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In septic shock, antibiotics and source control are as critical as fluid resuscitation. Fluids alone without addressing the infection is a critical error in this setting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate needle decompression of the chest</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Needle decompression treats obstructive shock from a tension pneumothorax. This patient's warm, flushed peripheries and infective source indicate distributive (septic) shock, not a mechanical obstruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: same framework, different treatment. Haemorrhagic shock needs blood and haemostasis; septic shock needs antibiotics, cultures, and source control alongside fluids."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The ABCDE framework is universal, but the cause-directed therapy changes with the mechanism — antibiotics and source control define septic shock management."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
